SECTION 48. Funds for Fishery Resource Management. The municipal government shall allocate all revenues derived from the utilization and exploitation of the municipal waters to fishery resources management activities such as but not limited to, fishery law enforcement, limited livelihood for fishers, research training and education.
SECTION 49. Share of Barangay on Revenues of the Municipal Government. The municipal government shall appropriate ten (10) percent of all fees derived from the utilization and exploitation of the municipal waters to the barangay: Provided, however, that the Municipal government, within 60 days from the enactment of this code, shall formulate implementing rules and regulations of this section: Provided, however, that such share shall be remitted to the Barangay before the end of each quarter, and shall be used the same purpose as of Municipal.
## ARTICLE XI
POST HARVEST FACILITIES, ACTIVITIES AND TRADES
SECTION 50. Establishment of Post- Harvest Facilities. The municipal government shall coordinate with the private sector and other concerned agencies and FARMC in the establishment of post-harvest facilities such as, but not limited to, municipal fish landing sites, fish ports, ice plants and cold storage and other fish processing establishment to serve primarily the needs of the municipal fishers.
SECTION 51. Municipal Fish Port Construction and Development. Municipal fish port development should be sited and designed in a manner that will minimize changes to existing water and sediment quality parameters such as salinity and temperature, dissolved oxygen, nitrogen, and sediment concentration, organic constituents, and transparency of the waters. Provided, that municipal fish ports and harbors should be placed in areas with the highest available flushing rate, and access rate, and access channels should be designed to minimize adverse water circulation changes and creation of stagnant water column; Provided, however, That municipal fish ports and harbors should incorporate facilities which allow for effective waste disposal and erosion control.
SECTION 52. Exportation and Importation of Fish and Fishery Product. Export of fish and fishery products shall be regulated whenever such exportation affects the food security and production; Provided, that exportation of live fish shall be prohibited except those which are hatched or propagated in accredited hatcheries and ponds. Provided, however, that to protect and maintain the local biodiversity or ensure the efficiency of supply, spawners, breeders, eggs and fry of bangus, prawn, and other endemic species, as may be determine by the MAGRO, shall not be exported or caused to be exported by any person; Provided, further, that no person shall import fish and fish products of whatever size, stage or form, for any purpose without securing the necessary permit.
SECTION 53. Auxiliary Invoice. All fish and fisheries products, except those caught in the violation of the provisions of the ordinances or are declared as health hazards by concerned institutions, must have an auxiliary invoice to be issued by the Municipal Agriculture office- Fishery Sector prior to their transport from the point of origin of their point of destination in the Philippines and/or export purposes upon payment of the prescribed fees to defray administrative costs therefor.
