# ENACTING AN ORDINANCE PROMULGATING LOCALIZED GUIDELINES FOR THE
CONTINUOUS PREVENTION OF THE CORONA VIRUS DISEASE 2019 (COVID-19) IN ALL
ESTABLISHMENTS AND OTHER WORK PLACES IN THE MUNICIPALITY OF STA. CRUZ,
DAVAO DEL SUR DURING THE DECLARATION OF THE NATIONAL PUBLIC HEALTH
EMERGENCY AND IMPOSING PENALTIES FOR VIOLATIONS THEREOF
WHEREAS, on March 8, 2020, President Rodrigo R. Duterte issued Proclamation No. 922,
entitled "Declaring a State of Public Health Emergency throughout the Philippines";
WHEREAS, on March 12, 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 a
"Pandemic";
WHEREAS, Proclamation No. 922 provides that all government agencies and local
Government Units (LGUs) are enjoined to render full assistance and cooperation and mobilize the
necessary resources to undertake critical, urgent, and appropriate response and measures in a timely
manner to curtail and eliminate the COVID-19;
WHEREAS, the 1987 Constitution mandates that the State shall protect and promote the right
to health of the people and instill health consciousness among them;
WHEREAS, additionally, it is also mandated that every local unit has to exercise the powers
expressly granted, those necessarily implied therefrom, as well as powers necessary, appropriate, or
incidental for its efficient and effective governance, and those which are essential to the promotion of
the general welfare;
WHEREAS, Sec. 447 (iv) of Republic Act No. 7160 provides that the sangguniang bayan, as
the legislative body of the municipality shall adopt measures to protect the inhabitants of the
municipality from the harmful effects of man-made or natural disasters and calamities and to provide
relief services and assistance for victims during and in the aftermath of said disasters or calamities
and their return to productive livelihood following said events;
WHEREAS, despite of the issuance of E.O. No. 28-2020 of the Municipality of Sta. Cruz
providing for rules and regulations to be observed under General Community Quarantine, there is a
need to further raise the level of disease prevention and control measures in all establishments and
other work places, especially, to limit, if not stop, the transmission of COVID-19;
WHEREAS, although, there is no confirmed case of COVID-19 in the Municipality of Sta. Cruz,
vigilance of all stakeholders with regard to the disease and how to prevent its transmission, are
indispensable mechanisms to combat COVID-19 even after the Enhanced Community Quarantine or
the General Community Quarantine has been lifted;
Municipal Ordinance No.05 | Page 1
NOW THEREFORE, on motion of Hon. Charlotte F. Gallego, unanimously seconded by the members present, it was
RESOLVED, as it is hereby resolved by the Sangguniang Bayan of Sta. Cruz, Davao del Sur, assembled in session to enact:
# MUNICIPAL ORDINANCE NO. 05
Series of 2020
# AN ORDINANCE PROMULGATING LOCALIZED GUIDELINES FOR THE CONTINUOUS PREVENTION OF THE CORONA VIRUS DISEASE 2019 (COVID-19) IN THE ALL ESTABLISHMENTS AND OTHER WORK PLACES IN THE MUNICIPALITY OF STA. CRUZ, DAVAO DEL SUR DURING THE DECLARATION OF THE NATIONAL PUBLIC HEALTH EMERGENCY AND IMPOSING PENALTIES FOR VIOLATIONS THEREOF
Be it ordained by the Sangguniang Bayan of Sta. Cruz, Davao del Sur, in session assembled that:
SECTION 1. Short Title. This Ordinance shall be known as the "LOCALIZED GUIDELINES FOR THE CONTINUOUS PREVENTION OF COVID-19 IN THE ALL ESTABLISHMENTS AND OTHER WORK PLACES IN THE MUNICIPALITY OF STA. CRUZ;
SECTION 2. Coverage. This Ordinance shall apply to all establishments and other work places that can lawfully conduct operations during the implementation of either Enhanced Community Quarantine, General Community Quarantine or mere Community Quarantine within the territorial jurisdiction of the Municipality of Sta. Cruz. This Ordinance shall likewise take effect until the State of Public Health Emergency has been lifted;
SECTION 3. Definition of Terms. The following terms used in this Ordinance shall mean:
A. Establishments – refer to all businesses, firms, companies, enterprises, corporations located within the municipality, to wit:
1) Establishments/factories/millers/depots/plants engaged in the manufacturing/processing/ warehouse of food and non-food products and health needs;
2) Establishments providing food/ water and other related necessities:
(i) supermarkets and markets, grocery stores, convenient stores;
(ii) Sari-sari stores;
(iii) Bakeshops;
(iv) Water-refilling stations;
(v) Cafeterias and cafes;
(vi) Refreshment parlors;
(vii) Restaurants; and
(viii) Food stalls.
3) Establishments providing for health services:
(i) Hospitals;
(ii) Medical clinics, diagnostic centers and laboratories;
(iii) Dental clinics;
(iv) Pharmacies and drug stores; and
(v) Therapeutic clinics.
4) Establishments providing for financial services:
(i) Banks;
(ii) Pawnshops;
(iii) Money transfer services; and
(iv) Payment and remittance centers.
Municipal Ordinance No.05 | Page 2
5) Establishments providing such other basic necessities and services:
(i) Agricultural supply stores;
(ii) Gasoline stations;
(iii) Beauty Parlor and Barbershops;
(iv) Tailoring/Dress shops;
(v) Liquefied Petroleum Gas (LPG) stores/outlets;
(vi) Funeral and crematory services;
(vii) Computer Services and Internet Cafes;
(viii) Hotels, lodges inns; and
(ix) Hardware and Supply
B. Other Work Places - refer to the offices, factories, shops and other places where people work, wherein social distancing must be implemented in order to prevent the spread of COVID-19.
SECTION 4. Guidelines on COVID-19 continued response in Establishments and Workplaces. The Municipality shall adopt the following documents released by national government, departments and agencies related to COVID-19 response in the workplace and other establishments:
Department of Health (DOH) Memorandum No. 2020-0056, February 3, 2020, Interim Guidelines for 2019 Novel Corona Virus Acute Respiratory Disease Response in the Workplace (See Annex "A");
Department of Health (DOH) Memorandum No. 2020-0056, February 3, 2020, Interim Guidelines for 2019 Novel Corona Virus Acute Respiratory Disease Response in Hotels and other similar establishments (See Annex "B");
Department of Health (DOH) Memorandum No. 2020-0072, February 3, 2020, Interim Guidelines for 2019 Novel Corona Virus Acute Respiratory Disease Response in Hospitals and Other Health Facilities (See Annex "C"); and
Department of Health (DOH) Administrative Order No. 2020-0015, April 27, 2020, Guidelines on the Risk-Based Public Health Standards for COVID-19 Mitigation (See Annex "D");
SECTION 5. Localized Guidelines. The following guidelines for the continued management of the COVID-19 shall be implemented:
A. Personal Protection and Hygiene: The following practices shall be observed to prevent or mitigate the spread of COVID-19 in all establishments and other work places:
(1) Ensure mandatory and proper use of facemasks or other similar protective paraphernalia and equipment within the premises of the establishment;
(2) Whenever practicable, hand-washing area or faucet must be installed in entrances;
(3) Maintain constant provision of hand sanitizers, alcohol and other disinfectants duly approved by the DOH with at least \(60\%\) ethanol or isopropanol;
(4) Provide thermal scanner to check the temperature of a person before entering the establishment or to institute screening procedures of sick or ill persons, and to create such referral systems to Municipal Health Office (MHO) and other medical centers in the Municipality;
(5) Encourage respiratory hygiene and cough etiquette by providing tissue within easy reach in all establishments and workstations, or by encouraging others to use handkerchief when coughing and to prohibit spitting within the premises of the establishments or workstations;
Provided that, Item (1) above shall be mandatory to all establishments and work places enumerated in Sec. 3 hereof;
Provided further that, item (2) above shall be mandatory to establishments and workplaces with high foot traffic such as factories/ millers/ depots/ plants engaged in the manufacturing/ processing/ storage of food and non-food products and hospitals. All other establishments or workplaces must provide hand sanitizers and dispensers with an alcohol-based solution at strategic entry points;
Municipal Ordinance No.05 | Page 3
B. Social Distancing Measures: The following practices shall be observed to prevent or mitigate the spread of COVID-19 in all establishments and other work places:
(1) Ensure observance of physical distancing by strictly maintaining a distance of at least one (1) meter radius between and among the people inside and within the premises of the establishment/work place. This distance should be observed even as to apparently healthy persons without symptoms. Provided that this section shall not be used as an excuse by the owner, manager, supervisor or any person who is directly in charge of the operations of the establishments and workplaces to terminate employment or dismiss an employee. In the event that maintaining one (1) meter radius of physical distancing is not practicable, the owner, manager, supervisor or any person who is directly in charge of the operations of the establishments and workplaces may construct an engineering control or physical interventions or modifications in their spaces or primary environments, that is meant to prevent the transmission of COVID19 (e.g. use of physical barriers, exhaust ventilation, etc.);
(2) Maintain "No Contact or No Handshaking Policy" at all times;
(3) Provide markers in entrances and exits distanced at 1 meter apart and to regulate the flow of ingress and egress;
(4) Create a floor plan, rearrange movables, mark the seats and floors to create at least 1 meter distance between employees, visitors, and clients;
(5) For establishments with high contact intensity among workers such as, factories/millers/depots/plants engaged in the manufacturing/processing/storage of food and non-food products, hospitals, and banks, offer telecommuting and replace in-person meetings in the workplace with video or telephone conference;
(6) Postpone or cancel mass gatherings until further advised by the DOH;
(7) Restaurants, fast food chains, food stalls, bakeshops, cafeterias, cafes and refreshment parlor shall be allowed to operate, provided that, the sale of food shall only be on a "take out" basis and "dine in" shall not be allowed, unless otherwise allowed by a National or Local directive. Owners may provide modified alternative structures and arrangements such as online ordering, drop-off points and delivery services;
(8) Once allowed to operate in a "dine-in" basis, restaurants, fast food chains, food stalls, bakeshops, cafeterias, cafes and refreshment parlor shall place marking tapes on the floor to guide individuals to stay at least one meter apart from each other when ordering food at the counter;
C. Environmental Measures: The following practices shall be observed to prevent or mitigate the spread of COVID-19 in all establishments and other work places:
(1) Clean frequently touched surfaces and objects, including doorknobs, tables, computer keyboards and others. All general facilities and all furnishings shall be cleaned with a 1 in 100 diluted household bleach (5.25%) solution and wiped at least once daily;
(2) Clean frequently common-used areas and those with touchpoints such as elevators, railings, staircases, light switches, and the like. The frequency of cleaning shall be stepped up if the facilities and furnishing become dirty;
(3) Make dispensers with alcohol-based hand rub available at public areas; and
(4) Install Foot Baths in all entrances using disinfectant solution of 1:10 bleach solution; 1 liter bleach mixed with nine liters of clean water;
Provided that, item (4) above shall be mandatory to all establishments and workplaces except for Sari-sari stores, food stalls, gasoline stations, and LPG stores/outlets.
D. Continued Information Dissemination: All establishments and workplaces are mandated to post at a noticeable area in their premises the DOH Advisories for Respiratory etiquette, Proper Wearing of Facemasks, Proper Hand Hygiene, and notice to observe physical distancing, copies thereof are attached hereto as Annexes "E" to "H", respectively.
The Municipal Health Office (MHO) together with the Barangay Health Emergency and Response Teams (BHERTS) shall conduct a massive information dissemination of this Ordinance together with related issuances and guidelines;
![img-0.jpeg](img-0.jpeg)
Municipal Ordinance No.05 | Page 4
To effectively promote and encourage compliance of this Ordinance, all establishments and workplaces shall conspicuously display at or near the entrance of the establishment/office a full copy of this Ordinance;
To ensure effective implementation of this part of the Ordinance, the COVID-19 Task Force together with the concerned committees of the Barangay shall conduct inspection and visitations to the establishments and offices aforementioned;
**SECTION 6. Punishable Acts.** This ordinance shall penalize the following persons and their corresponding acts:
A. Any owner, manager, supervisor or any person who is directly in charge of the operations of the establishments and workplaces who fails to come up and implement strategies or mechanisms to effectively carry out social distancing among his/her workforce and among customers, clients and visitors;
B. Any person, employer, employee, guests, clients, customer who does not observe social distancing despite of the instruction or notification from the manager, supervisor, security guard or any employee of the establishments and workplaces; and
C. Any owner, manager, supervisor, employee or any person who violates the mandatory provisions of Section 5 hereof;
**SECTION 7. Penalty Clause.** The following offenders of this ordinance and their corresponding acts shall be penalized, to wit:
FOR VIOLATIONS OF SEC. 6A AND 6B OF THIS ORDINANCE:
A. Any person, natural or juridical, who violates this ordinance shall be subjected to the fines and/or penalties hereunder set forth:
1) First Offense: Issuance of Notice of Ordinance Violation (NOV) and Reprimand;
2) Second Offense: A fine of One Thousand (P 1,000.00) Pesos or imprisonment for a period of five (5) days, or both in the discretion of the court.
3) Third and Succeeding Offenses: A Fine of One thousand Five Hundred (P 1,500.00) Pesos or imprisonment for a period of ten (10) days, or both in the discretion of the court. The business permit and license to operate of the concerned entity shall be suspended or cancelled after notice and hearing;
FOR VIOLATIONS OF SEC. 6C OF THIS ORDINANCE:
1) First Offense: Issuance of Notice of Ordinance Violation and Reprimand;
2) Second Offense: Issuance of \(2^{\text{nd}}\) Notice of Ordinance Violation and warning
3) Third and Succeeding Offenses A fine of One Thousand (P 500.00) Pesos or imprisonment for a period of five (5) days, or both in the discretion of the court.
**SECTION 8. Procedure In The Issuance Of Notice of Ordinance Violation (NOV).** The Following procedure shall be observed by any law enforcer in the apprehension of any person who violates this Ordinance:
a. Apprehending Officer must introduce himself or herself;
b. Issue the NOV indicating therein the name, address, and other pertinent data of the person apprehended, including the violation;
c. Inform the violator of his/her right to avail of the Offer to Compromise, as provided in Section 9, hereof, and ishall issue the corresponding OFFER TO COMPROMISE FORM;
**SECTION 9. Offer to Compromise.** Any person apprehended for violation of this ordinance, who wish not to contest the violation and is willing to pay voluntarily the administrative fine imposed upon him/her prior to the filling of formal complaint, shall be allowed to pay said fine with the Office of the Treasurer, to avoid being criminally prosecuted.
Municipal Ordinance No.05 | Page 5
Provided that Offer to Compromise shall only be applied to the second offenses for violations of sec. 6A and 6B of this ordinance and third offenses for violations of sec. 6C of this ordinance.
Provided further that, if the violator had already availed of the Offer to Compromise, three (3) times, he/she can no longer avail of the same and the corresponding complaint for the violation of the Ordinance, with the maximum penalty imposed should be filed against the violator;
**SECTION 10. Procedure in Offer to Compromise.**
a. The violator shall be given five (5) working days from the submission of OFFER TO COMPROMISE FORM within which to pay the administrative fine. For this purpose, the fines prescribed for in Section 7 hereof for second offenses for violations of sec. 6A and 6B and third offenses for violations of sec. 6C, shall be treated as administrative fines.
b. Administrative Fine shall be paid to the Office of the Treasurer of the Municipality. Thereafter, it shall issue the corresponding official receipt;
c. After payment of the fine, the violator shall present the Official Receipt to the apprehending enforcer, who shall attach a Certified True Copy of the receipt to the documents of the case and the case shall be considered closed;
**SECTION 11. Non-Payment of Administrative Fine.** If the violator of this ordinance fails or refuses to pay the administrative fine herein set forth, despite the submission of the form of offer to compromise, appropriate case shall be filed in court against the said violator.
**SECTION 12. Separability Clause.** Should any section or provision of this Ordinance be declared void and ineffective by final judgment of a competent judicial authority, the other provisions hereof, not affected thereby, shall continue to be in full force and effect.
**SECTION 13. Repealing Clause.** Any ordinance, resolution, executive order or local laws inconsistent herewith shall be deemed repealed, amended or modified accordingly.
**SECTION 14. Effectivity Clause** - This ordinance shall take effect 15 days following the completion of its publication.
**APPROVED**, by a unanimous vote of all the members of the Sangguniang Bayan present.
