# ENACTING MUNICIPAL ORDINANCE ENTITLED "MT. APO NATURAL PARK VIA SIBULAN TRAIL MOUNTAINEERING AND TREKKING ORDINANCE OF 2003"
WHEREAS, Mt. Apo, the country's highest peak, is a natural park subject to the implementing rules and regulations of the National Integrated Protected Areas System (NIPAS) Law of 1992 which declares that its use and enjoyment must be consistent with the principle of biological diversity and sustainable development;
WHEREAS, Sta. Cruz is one of LGUs that is tasked to protect this natural wealth through regulation of activities to be conducted therein like mountaineering and trekking activities;
WHEREAS, Sibulan Trail is located in Sta. Cruz and it is one of the recognized trail for such activities which shall be covered by a regulatory ordinance;
WHEREFORE, on motion of Hon. Lolita P. Legaspi, unanimously seconded, it was
RESOLVED, as it is hereby resolved by the Sangguniang Bayan of Sta. Cruz, assembled in session to enact the following ordinance, to wit:
# MUNICIPAL ORDINANCE NO. 08
Series of 2003
# MT. APO NATURAL PARK VIA SIBULAN TRAIL
MOUNTAINEERING AND TREKKING
ORDINANCE OF 2003
Be it ordained by the Sangguniang Bayan of Sta. Cruz, that
Section 1 - TITLE. - This ordinance shall be known as Mt. Apo Natural Park Via Sibulan Trail Mountaineering and Trekking Ordinance of 2003;
Section 2 - DECLARATION OF POLICY - Pursuant to Republic Act No. 7586 otherwise known as the National Integrated Protected Areas System (NIPAS) Law of 1992 and its implementing rules and regulations, this ordinance declares that:
2.1 the use and enjoyment of protected areas such as the Mt. Apo Natural Park must be consistent with the principles of biological diversity and sustainable development;
1
2.2 the effective administration of protected areas is possible only through cooperation among national and local government units and concerned private organizations, and, as such the law mandates the institutionalization of the Protected Area Management Board (PAMB) in areas designated as protected areas such as the Mt. Apo Natural Park;
**Section 3 – DEFINITION OF TERMS.** For purposes of this ordinance, the following terminologies/acronyms shall mean as follows:
3.1 Sibulan Trail – shall mean the PAMB- declared official trail to Mt. Apo Natural Park located in the jurisdiction of Sta. Cruz that includes Barangay Sibulan and Sitios of Tudaya, Colan, Garuk and Tinikaran;
3.2 Mt. Apo Natural Park – refers to the volcanic mountain of Mt. Apo in Southern Mindanao known as the “country’s highest peak”;
3.3 Mountaineering – refers to an undertaking carried out with the intent/purpose of reaching the summit of Mt. Apo. The use of technical equipments, guides and porters and the psychological as well as physical desire to reach the summit under any weather conditions determine such an undertaking;
3.4 Trekking – refers to an undertaking by slowly moving on foot along Sibulan Trail for purposes of enjoying the pristine ambiance of the Mt. Apo Natural Park not necessarily reaching its peak;
3.5 PAMB – refers to the Protected Area Management Board, a multi-sectoral body organized to administer the protected area as duly mandated by NIPAS Act;
3.6 PASu – refers to the Protected Area Superintendent who acts as DENR Chief Operating Officer in the Protected Area;
3.7 Sta. Cruz PAIC – refers to Sta. Cruz Provincial Agri-Industrial Center office;
3.8 Climbing Permit – a legal document required of a mountaineer/trekker before he can undertake said activity in the Mt. Apo Natural Park;
3.9 Sibulan Trail Guide – refers to an individual accredited as such by the Sangguniang Bayan of Sta. Cruz and PAMB, exercising the profession of guiding tourists, both domestic or foreign, to the Mt. Apo Natural Park, within the jurisdiction of Sta. Cruz;
3.10 Sibulan Porter – an individual exercising portering services by carrying baggages, cleaning and maintenance work in mountain and forest areas for tourists both foreign and domestic for a fee, commission or any form of lawful remuneration duly recognized through accreditation by the Sangguniang Bayan of Sta. Cruz;
3.11 MANP – acronym for Mt. Apo Natural Park;
2
3.12 Tenured Migrant – refers to a resident of MANP who has been actually living in the area for at least six (6) months;
Section 4 – SCOPE AND COVERAGE - This ordinance shall cover the following pertinent concerns, to wit:
4.1 mountaineering activities as defined in the guidelines stipulated in this ordinance but which that covers only the jurisdiction of this municipality;
4.2 trekking or mountain climbing activities at the Mt. Apo Natural Park via the Sibulan Trail regardless of purpose by individuals or groups;
4.3 guiding and portering activities within the jurisdiction of this municipality to include accreditation procedures of mountain and tourist guides by the Sangguniang Bayan of Sta. Cruz;
Section 5 – OBJECTIVES - This ordinance shall be made the basis for the regulatory implementation of all mountaineering and trekking or mountain climbing activities at the Mt. Apo Natural Park via the Sibulan Trail;
It shall also set the guidelines on trekking activities in order to:
5.1 minimize the negative impact related with trekking activities, to the fragile ecosystem and the cultural fabric of the people in the area, such as further expansion of campsites, cutting down of trees for camping purposes, disposal of solid wastes, vandalism, erosion of the trails and disturbance of wildlife among others;
5.2 limit the number of trekkers in a particular time in due recognition of the carrying capacity of the area;
5.3 install and educate the trekkers that their visit to the area contributes to the conservation and development of the park by paying fees, which shall be used for the protection, conservation, development and visitor management of the park within its jurisdiction;
5.4 give value to trekking activities because Mt. Apo is a protected area and it has ecological, economic, social and cultural values to be considered;
5.5 develop compatible, applicable and ecologically sensitive visitor's management program to include provision of the basic facilities and services;
5.6 promote educational activities that shall further advance the principles of biodiversity conservation and sustainable development of the park;
5.7 ensure the safety of the trekkers and to make trekking activities worthwhile, memorable and enjoyable.
Similarly, for purposes of accrediting guides and porters, this ordinance shall:
5.8 develop a system on the effective delivery of service of porters and guides at the MANP within the jurisdiction of Sta. Cruz and which is consistent with the biodiversity conservation values of the area;
3
5.9 uplift and professionalize the standards of guiding and portering especially that of the locales in the area;
5.10 develop accreditation scheme for guides and porters at the MANP, within Sta. Cruz;
5.11 provide opportunity to qualified tenured migrants with alternative livelihood in the exercise of the guiding and portering profession;
5.12 provide accreditation to other individuals allowed by law in the exercise of guiding profession;
5.13 integrate ecologically sound practices in nature-based tourism in MANP.
Section 6 – INGRESS/EGRESS POINTS – as officially declared by the PAMB, entry point for Sta. Cruz, Davao del Sur to Mt. Apo Natural Park is via Sibulan Trail. Sitio Colan along the Sibulan Trail is the exit station however it may also serve as the entry point.
Section 7 – CARRYING CAPACITY - The number of participants for organized climb to Mt. Apo via Sibulan Trail shall be a maximum carrying capacity of 250 mountaineers per climb;
Section 8 – CLIMBING SEASON - Mt. Apo via Sibulan Trail is open to mountaineering undertakings only in the months of March to end of May and beginning of October to end of December;
However, in the event that Philippines Atmospheric Geophysical Services Administration (PAGASA) declares weather disturbances, and/or storm signal, Mt. Apo Natural park within Sta. Cruz jurisdiction shall automatically be closed for mountaineering activities.
# Section 9 – MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS
9.1 Climbing Permit. A climbing permit is required before any mountaineering activity can be undertaken in the Mt. Apo Natural Park via Sibulan Trail which shall be governed by the following:
9.1.1 All mountaineering groups shall communicate in writing their intent/desire to undertake mountaineering activities in Mt. Apo via Sibulan Trail with the Office of Sta. Cruz Provincial Agri-Industrial Center (PAIC), a month prior to the proposed date of climb;
9.1.2 An official application form can be acquired from the PAIC Office by any concerned party to personally fill up the forms and accomplish the requirements and return the same to the PAIC Office;
9.1.3 The PAIC Office shall evaluate, process and recommend to the PAMB for the approval of the application;
9.1.4 The climbing permit shall be issued only by the PASU or Authorized Issuing Officer upon approval of the application by the PAMB, subject to a first come first serve basis;
9.1.5 The applicant(s) shall then be given the necessary briefing and orientation on the features of the park
4
including rules and regulations, safety measures and other relevant information as provided for in the visitors manual of the Mt. Apo Natural Park;
9.1.6 The applicant(s) shall then execute waiver of responsibility. Minors or children below 18 years old are required parental waiver.
9.1.7 The applicant(s) shall then pay the required fees as provided for in this ordinance;
9.1.8 The PASu or Authorized Issuing Officer issues the permit and the permittee shall then pay the permit fee to the local treasury of Sta. Cruz, Davao del Sur;
## 9.2 Terms and Conditions of the Permit
9.2.1 No one is allowed to go into trekking to Mt. Apo Natural Park via Sibulan Trail without the approved permit from the PASu or Authorized Issuing Officer;
9.2.2 The permittee(s) shall check-in at the entrance station and present the permit for inspection by the Authorized or Deputized Park Personnel. The permittee(s) is/are required to present their luggage/s and items brought to check up the necessary equipments for trekking such as tent, sleeping bags, camping stoves, food supply, medicine kit, communication system, hard hats, etc.;
9.2.3 Foreign nationals and first timers in the area are required to have an accredited guide. Maximum of five trekkers for one guide is allowed. No one is allowed to trek alone; the minimum is at least a group of three (3);
9.2.4 Only the official trails and designated campsites shall be used for trekking and camping purposes to minimize the impact of trekking activities to the habitat of the wildlife, and for the purposes of monitoring and safety of the trekkers;
9.2.5 The trekkers shall keep the areas clean and sanitary at all times. Garbage and other refuse shall be disposed of properly outside the park or be deposited at the exit station;
9.2.6 Upon exit at the park, the permittee shall report at the station for proper information and guidance of the park authorities;
9.2.7 The Local Government Unit and PAMB-DENR are not liable for any loss/damage of the personal properties/belongings, injury or death due to accident that may happen to any trekker(s) on the duration of the trek at the park;
9.3 Mountaineering/Trekking Permit. To achieve the objectives of this ordinance, the Protected Area Management Board through the Local Government Unit of Sta. Cruz shall issue a permit based on the purpose and the area of visit in the Mt. Apo Natural Park covered within the territorial jurisdiction of Sta. Cruz;
9.3.1 Mountaineering/Trekking permit from the entrance station of Barangay Sibulan up to Sitio Colan;
5
9.3.2 Mountaineering/Trekking permit from barangay Sibulan entrance station up to the summit/peak of Mt. Apo;
9.3.3 Mountaineering/Trekking permit from Sitio Colan to Mt. Apo via Kapatagan.
9.4 **Mountaineering/Trekking Permit Fee.** A permit fee shall be imposed in all mountaineering/trekking activities in the area approval of which shall be governed by the following:
9.4.1 All approved applications for mountaineering/trekking activities from Sibulan to Mt. Apo Park are subject to payment of permit fees in the amount of Five Hundred Pesos (P 500.00);
9.4.2 All approved applications for mountaineering/trekking activities to Sitio Colan only shall pay Three Hundred Pesos (P300.00);
9.4.3 All approved applications for mountaineering/trekking activities from Sitio Colan to Mt. Apo Park via Kapatagan shall pay Three Hundred Pesos (P300.00) only;
9.4.4 Collections and remittances for this purpose shall be divided accordingly:
| IPAF-DENR | - | 20% |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Municipal | - | 30% |
| Province | - | 10% |
| Barangay | - | 40% |
9.4.5 The funds collected for the purpose shall be deposited in the trust fund of the Local government Unit and shall be used for the maintenance of the trails and campsites and rehabilitation/restoration project of the Mt. Apo Natural Park;
9.4.6 Activities pertaining to general management, administration, monitoring, evaluation and biodiversity conservation related efforts initiated or co-sponsored by the PAMB-DENR and Local Government Unit of Sta. Cruz are exempted from trekking fees;
In case of co-sponsorship however, the said activity shall be covered with an agreement;
9.4.7 Accredited guides and porters of the LGU of Sta. Cruz shall be exempted from trekking fees should they be accompanying visitors/mountaineers;
9.4.8 The SAVERS Rescue Team shall also be exempted from trekking fees should they render services in organized climbs;
9.5 **Route Itinerary.** There shall a route itinerary the group must follow in the course of their mountaineering and trekking activities;
9.5.1 The mountaineering group shall determine its own itinerary as reflected in their application form. The group is not authorized to change/alter the approved route/itinerary without proper authorization from the PAMB.
6
9.5.2 The PAMB reserves the right to change/alter the route/itinerary of any particular climb as it may deem necessary or as circumstances so warrants.
9.6 Climbing Requirements. There shall be required prescribed equipments in mountaineering and trekking activities to Mt. Apo Natural Park.
9.6.1 All mountaineers intending to climb Mt. Apo are required to provide themselves with but not limited to the following basic mountaineering equipments:
9.6.1a Group tents
9.6.1d First Aid Kit
9.6.1b Sleeping Bag
9.6.1e Hard Hat
9.6.1c Camping Stove
9.6.1f Lantern/flashlight
9.7 Employment of Guides and Porters
9.7.1 All mountaineering group are required to be accompanied by at least one Sibulan Trail guide for a group of five (5). No climb shall be allowed without an accompanying official guide to climb Mt. Apo.
9.7.2 Payment for basic services of guide and porters shall be given directly to the concerned party based on legal fees.
9.7.3 The hiring of porter services is optional on the part of the mountaineer. However, for purposes of convenience and to increase the margin of safety and security measures, it is recommended to mountaineers and trekkers to hire porters services.
9.7.4 The mountaineers shall hire accredited Guides and Porters within Sibulan. However, should there be no available porters or guides in the community, the mountaineers may employ the services of those available at the neighboring community.
9.8 Field Rations. All mountaineers to Mt. Apo Natural Park passing through the Sibulan Trail are required to provide themselves with their own field rations subject to inspection and approval of PAMB thru LGU-Sta. Cruz authorized personnel.
9.9 Waste Disposal. Wastes must be disposed of properly by mountaineers and trekkers in the following manner:
9.9.1 Field rations brought along by the mountaineers shall be inspected and inventoried for garbage purposes;
9.9.2 Mountaineers are required to carry down their disposed garbage as per inventory and deposit at the exit station in Sitio Colan. Employed porters shall assist on this matter.
9.9.3 Mountainers who fail to bring down his disposed garbage shall be fined according to the value of goods as inspected and inventoried at the start of the activity;
9.9.4 All approved mountaineering activities shall properly and strictly observe the basic rules and ethics on environment;
![img-0.jpeg](img-0.jpeg)
7
**9.10 Medical Certificate.** Upon registration, the mountaineer shall submit their latest medical certificate by competent physician to ensure that they are physically fit to join the mountaineering activities.
**9.11 Waiver of Responsibility.** The Local Government Unit of Sta. Cruz and the PAMB-DENR are not to be held liable nor responsible for any untoward incident(s), accident(s) that may result to injury or death as a consequence of circumstances beyond human control or force majeure or otherwise directly or indirectly due to negligence on the part of the mountaineer or any delays or cause the abortion/cancellation of the duly approved climb due to natural agents.
9.11.1 All fees already made and paid to the treasury of local government and/or any of its authorized representative are non-refundable.
**9.12 Closure of Trekking Activities.** The Local Government Unit of Sta. Cruz may recommend to the PAMB-DENR the areas within its territorial jurisdiction for closure to trekking activities for reasons deemed necessary for biodiversity conservation, safety of visitors/tourists and if the situation so warrants the closure of the area for such activity;
However, once volcanic alert level shall be announced by the PHIVOLCS and the PAGASA declares storm signal, automatically Mt. Apo shall be closed for trekking activities until further notice.
**SECTION 10 - Porters and Guides.** An accreditation scheme for Sibulan Trail porters and guide shall be established to develop a system for effective delivery of their services at the park and shall lay down guidelines and policies on this matter;
**10.1 Qualification of Sibulan Trail Guides and Porters.** This ordinance shall set the qualifications for guides and porters which shall be made the basis for issuance of permits and exemptions, if any, to mountaineering and trekking activities;
**10.1.1 Sibulan Trail guides shall possess the following qualifications:**
- 10.1.1a Resident Filipino Citizen
- 10.1.1b Preferably a tenured migrant of Barangay Sibulan and/or resident of adjoining areas within the Mt. Apo Natural Park;
- 10.1.1c At least eighteen (18) years old;
- 10.1.1d Physically and mentally fit;
- 10.1.1e With good moral character in the community he/she is residing;
- 10.1.1f Preferably can speak and understand English;
- 10.1.1g Must be fully familiar on the different aspects of the park that include the trail, camps, water sources, scenic spots, history and biodiversity. Preference shall be given to those who are already exercising guiding and portering services at the park;
**10.1.2 Sibulan Porters must have the following qualifications:**
- 10.1.2a Resident Filipino citizen;
- 10.1.2b A tenured migrant of Barangay Sibulan and/or resident of the adjoining areas of
8
the MANP;
10.1.2c At least eighteen (18) years old;
10.1.2d Physically and mentally fit;
10.1.2e Of good moral character in the community he/she is residing;
10.1.2f Preferably can speak and understand English;
10.1.2g Must be fully familiar of the different aspects of the park that include trail, camps, water sources, scenic spots and biodiversity;
# **10.2 Procedures for the Application, Processing and Approval of Accreditation.** Accreditation of guides and porters for mountaineering and trekking activities in the MANP via Sibulan Trail must follow prescribed procedures promulgated in this ordinance.
**10.2.1** For tenured migrants and/or resident of adjoining areas of Mt. Apo Natural Park applying for accreditation as guides and porters shall submit the following:
10.2.1a Letter of Intent
10.2.1b Accomplished detailed resume' with colored 2 x 2 (passport size) photo
10.2.1c Medical certificate issued by any competent physician;
10.2.1d Certification of residency and good standing in the community issued by the Barangay Captain or Mayor;
10.2.1e Experience/record of rendering related services related to mountain guiding and portering;
10.2.1f Sworn statement that LGU-Sta. Cruz of PAMB-DENR has no responsibility for whatever in their guiding and portering work;
10.2.1g Payment of One Hundred Pesos (P100.00) professional fee as guides/porters;
10.2.1h Other requirements that the PAMB require from time to time as the need arises;
**10.2.2** For non-tenured migrants seeking accreditation in addition to the above-mentioned requirements. They shall also submit certification that he/she is a bona fide member of the duly registered local mountaineering association and/or Mountaineering Federation of the Philippines and with experience/record of rendering services related to the performance of mountain guiding and/or certification that he/she is a licensed mountaineer or national guide;
**10.2.3** Following the submission of the requirements for accreditation, the PAIC Office shall immediately conduct background investigation and to submit the findings and recommendation to the SB Committee on Eco-Tourism for appropriate action;
**10.2.4** The Sangguniang Bayan will then resolve the accreditation or non-accreditation of the applicant;
**10.2.5** The accreditation papers following its approval will only be released upon presentation of an official receipt as payment of professional fee;
9
10.2.6 Accredited guides and porters will be required to attend orientation seminar and other necessary trainings which shall be conducted by Sta. Cruz PAIC Office;
10.3 Privileges of Accredited Sibulan Guiders and Porters. Accredited guides and porters shall have the following privileges:
10.3.1 Exemption from entrance and trekking fees while exercising their profession. However, in the event that they shall engage in trekking activities without accompanying visitors, they shall be charged with required fees;
10.3.2 Provision of necessary capability enhancement activities like skills training, seminar workshop to further promote their services;
10.3.3 Assistance in their organization and registration of their association;
10.3.4 Fund accessing for livelihood projects;
10.4 Validity of Accreditation. Accreditation shall only be valid for one (1) year from the date of its approval;
10.5 Renewal. Thirty (30) days or one (1) month before the expiration of the accreditation, the accredited guide and porter shall submit to the PAIC Office the letter of intent for the renewal of his license with the following requirements;
10.5.1 Application for Renewal
10.5.2 Rating Sheet from clients
10.5.3 Recent Medical Certificate
10.5.4 Guide/Porter performance monitoring and evaluation report of the President of their association, PAIC Office personnel and Park personnel;
10.5.5 Payment of prescribed fees;
10.5.6 Attendance to the refresher course or re-orientation seminar-workshop conducted annually;
All of above requirements shall be the basis for the re-accreditation by the Sangguniang Bayan;
10.6 Responsibility of the Accredited Sibulan Trails and Porters. An accredited guide and porter shall the role of a steward of the environment that they travel and work in. Apart from professionalizing the community tour guiding services, guides and porters must be able to integrate environment values, education and pleasure as the ultimate goal in every activity. As such, responsibilities of guides for Mt. Apo are clearly defined into the following specific areas of concerns:
10.6.1 Environmental Care- Sibulan Trail guides and porters must have high respect for the Mt. Apo Natural Park and give due care to it not because they are dependent on it for their livelihood, but in the pursuit of really caring the park and ensuring pleasure and safety of its clients and visitors and encourage the latter to respect the park and be responsible for its future use and to utilize the area with the least possible impact to the environment and preserve a more natural state than they found it;
10.6.2 Food Packing – Prepare a ready suggestion or plan on meals to avoid unnecessary leftovers. An additional of
![Handwritten signature or mark at the bottom of the page.]()
10
one day food requirements is highly suggested for emergency cases;
10.6.3 Disposal of Food Waste. Wastewater shall be separated from food wastes. Observe at least 50 meters distance from any water source. Solid waste should be properly sealed and carried out of the park;
10.6.4 Cleaning Pots and Utensils. Pots and utensils must be not be immersed in any natural water sources;
10.6.5 Personal Washing. Food must always be handled with clean hands, wash hands especially after urinating and defecating.
10.6.6 Disposal of Human Waste. Always observe distance from any water sources when disposing human wastes while on trails or in camps;
10.6.7 Garbage Disposal. The Sibulan Trail guide and porter make an effort to pick up garbage left by irresponsible tourists/trekkers. All garbage and other refuse shall be hang at night to avoid attack by small animals;
Section 11 - FINDER'S INCENTIVE. An incentive of FIVE HUNDRED PESOS (P500.00) finders fee shall be given to any individuals who can report to the LGU any Mountaineers/Trekkers who have no approved trekking permit or found to be in violation to the above provisions.
The finder's fee shall be taken from the fines as provided in this ordinance.
Section 12 - PROHIBITED ACTS. As covered by the NIPAS Law, the following acts are prohibited within the Mt. Apo Natural Park;
12.1 Hunting, destroying, or mere possession of any plants and animals or products derive therefrom without any permit from the PAMB-DENR;
12.2 Dumping any waste products detrimental to the Protected Area, or to the plants and animals or inhabitants therein;
12.3 Mutilating, defacing or destroying objects of natural beauty or objects of interest to cultural communities of scenic value;
12.4 Damaging and leaving roads and trails in damaged condition;
12.5 Leaving in exposed or unsanitary conditions refuse or debris, or depositing in the ground or in bodies of water, and;
12.6 Altering, removing, destroying or defacing boundary marks and signs;
12.7 Leaving marks on tree trunks, rocks, boulders and other natural structures;
12.8 Climbing/trekking without the approved permit from the PAMB-DENR or its authorized issuing Officer;
Section 13 - PENALTY CLAUSE - The penalties provided for in the NIPAS Law shall be imposed to whoever violates the law and provided further that the PAMB is empowered to exercise its powers and functions over the entire park. The LGU of Sta. Cruz shall be constrained to strictly implement the provision stipulated in this ordinance.
11
Penalties for violations of any or all of the provisions stipulated in this rules and regulations shall be imposed with fines of not less than TWO THOUSAND PESOS (P 2,000.00) and imprisonment of not exceeding Six (6) months and be blacklisted and banned from mountaineering, trekking and/or climbing Mt. Apo Natural Park in all entries for two (2) years to start from the time the penalty is imposed.
Section 14 – SEPARABILITY CLAUSE. The provisions of this ordinance is hereby declared separable and on the event the said provisions or any part thereof is declared invalid by any competent court of law, the validity of all other provisions of this guidelines shall not be affected thereby.
Section 15 – AMENDMENT. This ordinance may be amended wholly or in part by the Sangguniang Bayan of Sta. Cruz, Davao del Sur through public notification,
Section 16 – EFFECTIVITY. This ordinance shall take effect 10 days after its approval and posting in three (3) conspicuous places and/or publication in a local newspaper.
